--- body ---
## Context

"Resists red team" cannot be a green checkmark, and it cannot be closed by a
one-shot audit — security is a **standing QA process**, not a boolean or a
single report. And the threat model here is **maximal, not small**: the
binary is *embarked in thousands of repos*, committed and travelling with
every project that adopts git-erg, executing in their pre-commit hooks and CI.
That makes it a **supply-chain and mass-RCE target** — one vuln, or one
poisoned binary, is exploitable across every adopter at once. Treat it that
way.

The work: a maximal threat model, a falsifiable hardening subset wired into
CI, and a **repeatable, AI-assisted red-team process** (re-run per release and
per change to the relevant surface), with a human gate on findings and on
threat-model changes.

## Threat model (maximal — a binary in thousands of repos)

- **Supply chain / reproducible builds (keystone).** The committed
  `tickets/erg` is an opaque blob in thousands of repos. Anyone must be able
  to **rebuild it bit-for-bit from `src/go/`** (pinned Go version,
  `CGO_ENABLED=0 -trimpath`, build stamp the only intended variance) and
  verify the committed binary matches — else a poisoned commit could swap the
  binary undetectably. An unverifiable committed binary *is* the liability.
  *Building block already present:* `erg version` prints the binary's SHA-256
  + the build revision (`selfHash`, `version.go`), and `update` already
  hash-compares. Two requirements on the displayed hash and how we teach its
  use:

  1. **Widely-available algorithm.** Keep **SHA-256** — every platform
     recomputes it with stock tools (`sha256sum`, `shasum -a 256`,
     `openssl dgst -sha256`, `certutil` on Windows). Name the algorithm in
     `erg version` and expose the **full** digest (the 12-char prefix is a
     friendly fingerprint, not a verification value), so a user can recompute
     and compare without guesswork.
  2. **Kindly remind the user of verification opsec** — two tiers, a gentle
     cluestick, not a nag:
     - *Basic:* recompute locally (`sha256sum tickets/erg`) and check it
       agrees with the self-reported `erg version` hash and the hash on a
       **maintainer-signed git release tag** (`git tag -s`, verified with
       `git verify-tag` / `--show-signature`). A bare hash is only as
       trustworthy as its channel; a signed tag is how git itself publishes
       trust — git-native, no bespoke manifest/revocation, no new dependency.
       (We considered a signed manifest + revocation list; closed YAGNI in
       0153 — that is not how git/Junio Hamano do it, and we have no observed
       compromise to justify the key-management cost.)
     - *Advanced:* don't trust the blob at all — have a powerful AI review the
       in-repo `src/go/`, rebuild it locally (git+go, **offline**), hash the
       rebuild, and confirm it matches the committed binary. `make verify`
       automates the rebuild+diff; the AI source review is the human/AI step.

  All of this is local and offline by design (`src/go/` travels in the repo;
  no phone-home) and discoverable (`erg version` points at it; README /
  integration.md document both tiers). Wording + discoverability are
  UX-reviewed (0152).
- **Update channel.** `erg update` (today `net/http`; 0148 → `git fetch`)
  must not be coercible into installing an attacker binary — integrity
  (hash/signature), trusted origin, no silent downgrade / MITM. A compromised
  update propagates to every repo that runs it.
- **Mass code-execution surface.** erg runs in hooks/CI everywhere; a parser
  RCE or command-injection is exploitable across all adopters simultaneously.
- **Untrusted `.erg` input.** Anyone can commit / PR a `.erg`; hooks/CI parse
  it. No RCE, **path traversal** (crafted ID/path-ref escaping `tickets/`),
  or **DoS** (huge file, deep nesting, ReDoS in the parser).
- **Path / ID injection.** IDs and path-refs feed filesystem paths
  (`resolveDir`, `resolveTicketByID`, `new`/`rm`/`archive`) — no `..`,
  absolute-path, or symlink escape.
- **Secret hygiene.** erg never reads, writes, or logs secrets; never leaks
  env/keys into tickets or output ("never leak keys").

Every finding carries a **severity multiplier**: it ships to thousands of
repos, so "low" is rarely low.

## QA process (repeatable, AI-assisted)

- **Falsifiable subset in CI** (every push): reproducible-build check (rebuild
  + byte-diff against committed `tickets/erg`); path-traversal fixtures
  rejected; input-DoS bounds; update-integrity (hash mismatch refuses).
- **AI-assisted red-team pass** (per release / per relevant change): an agent
  works a red-team checklist — craft malicious refs/IDs, oversized/ReDoS
  inputs, attempt an update hijack in a sandbox — and reports. Cheap to
  re-run, so run it often.
- **Human gate:** review findings, approve threat-model changes, set severity.
  High-severity findings become their own fix tickets.

## Relevant files

- Reproducibility & verification: `Makefile` build flags (0147 — pinned Go,
  `CGO_ENABLED=0 -trimpath`), a `make verify` target, CI rebuild + diff;
  `version.go` (name SHA-256, expose the full digest) / `update.go`
  (`selfHash`); a published reference hash (releases / out-of-band); and
  README / integration.md (the two opsec tiers + the cluestick).
- Parser + ref resolution: `erg.go`, `ref.go`, `main.go`
  (`resolveDir`/`resolveTicketByID`).
- Path construction: `new.go`, `rm.go`, `archive.go`.
- `update.go` (coordinate update-integrity with 0148).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Maximal threat model documented (supply-chain framing + severity
      multiplier).
- [ ] Committed `tickets/erg` is byte-reproducible from source; a CI check
      rebuilds the recorded revision and diffs it (reusing `selfHash`).
- [ ] `erg version` names the algorithm (SHA-256) and exposes the full digest;
      the displayed hash is recomputable with stock tools.
- [ ] Release tags are signed (git-native `git tag -s`) and the verify step
      (`git verify-tag`) is documented — this is the trustable publication.
      No bespoke manifest/revocation (0153 closed YAGNI).
- [ ] Local + offline advanced path: `make verify` rebuilds the recorded
      revision and diffs against the committed binary (git+go only, no network).
- [ ] Both opsec tiers are documented and the user is kindly pointed at them
      (cluestick), not left to find them.
- [ ] Falsifiable hardening tests in CI (path-traversal, input-DoS,
      update-integrity), each with a negative control.
- [ ] An AI-assisted red-team checklist exists and has run once; the process
      + cadence are documented so it re-runs — not one-and-done.
- [ ] Findings recorded; high-severity items filed as fix tickets.

## Children (automatable subset — raid 151)

- **0155** — `erg version`: full SHA-256, name algorithm, verification hint
- **0156** — `make verify`: reproducible-build rebuild+byte-diff, Go toolchain pin
- **0157** — Security hardening tests: path-traversal, input-DoS, negative controls
- **0158** — Threat model doc, red-team checklist, opsec tier documentation

## Deferred to human (stays on 0151)

- Signed release tags (`git tag -s`) — requires maintainer GPG key
- Threat-model formal sign-off
- Red-team findings severity assignment

## Notes

- **needs-human** (AGENTS.md §2 security gate). The "never leak keys" /
  red-team half; data-safety *integrity* is 0149.
- The **reproducible-build verification** is falsifiable and could be split
